    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d684">

                <pb facs="0894" n="884" xml:id="pg884"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.20 Missions/9–847</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chief of the Army Advisory Group in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lucas</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Ambassador in
                        China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>)<anchor n="51"
                        xml:id="d684-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-09-05">5 September
                        1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Your Excellency</hi>: In my letter to you of 28 June
                        1947<anchor n="52" xml:id="d684-f2"/> regarding the assessment of the
                    character and extent of the Army Advisory Group, I discussed at length my
                    thought regarding additional training for the Chinese Armed Forces.</p>
                <p>Due to the vital importance of this training and after a detailed study of
                        [SM–4992],<anchor n="53" xml:id="d684-f3"/> I feel that advice and
                    assistance to Division staffs on organization and training is essential and
                    within the contemplated activities of this Group. Consequently, I plan to send
                    Advisory Teams to such Division Training Centers as the Ministry of National
                    Defense may establish south of Yangtze or in Formosa.</p>
                <p>Any Division Training Centers south of the Yangtze or in Formosa would be removed
                    from the combat zone and from Communist dominated areas, thus exempting these
                    locations from the very general restriction imposed by General Marshall orally
                    to members of this Group before his departure from China.</p>
                <p>Realizing that a program of this type undertaken by this Group will lead to
                    criticism in the press and radio from certain quarters, I intend to advise the
                    War Department of my plans for these Advisory Teams. Prior to my communicating
                    with the War Department, I would appreciate your comments on the political
                    aspect or implications of this plan, so your thoughts may be forwarded to the
                    War Department for their information.</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">John</hi> P. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Lucas</hi>
                    </signed>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Maj. Gen., USA</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
